course: everyday
lesson: 241
title: Safe toys for you and little ones
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Toys are made for fun, but some are dangerous for babies and toddlers, who put everything in their mouths. Small parts, marbles, Lego bricks, balloons and loose toy eyes can choke a child under three, which is why toys carry age labels such as «3+».
explain: Two tiny things are especially dangerous. A swallowed button battery can burn through the throat within hours, and two swallowed magnets can stick together through the gut. Both are emergencies.
explain: Big children's toys need sense too: drive a remote-control car away from the street, fly a drone only where allowed, aim water guns at the body, not the face, and never take a realistic toy gun to a public place.
explain: Example: a boy in Manchester finds a button battery under the sofa where his baby sister crawls. He gives it to his dad at once, and they tape the toy's battery cover shut.
fact: Keep small parts, marbles, Lego bricks and balloons away from children under three, because small pieces can choke a baby or toddler.
fact: A swallowed button battery can burn through the throat within hours, so give a loose one to an adult at once; swallowing one is an emergency.
fact: Two swallowed magnets can stick together through the gut and cause serious injury, so pick up all small strong magnets.
fact: Age labels such as «3+» warn about choking and other dangers for young children.
fact: Stop playing with a toy with a broken sharp edge and show an adult, because it can cut skin or poke an eye.
fact: Drive a remote-control car only in the yard, park or a quiet path, never in the street, because you might run after it into traffic.
fact: Fly a toy drone only where allowed, with an adult, away from people, roads, airports and animals, because propellers can cut.
fact: Keep strings and cords away from babies and never put a cord around anyone's neck, because cords can strangle.
fact: Keep ride-on toys and scooters away from pools and stairs, because a child can roll into the water or fall down steps.
fact: Aim water guns at the body, not the face, and only at people who are playing too.
fact: Never take a realistic toy gun to a public place, because people, including police, can mistake it for a real gun.
fact: Play with slime and play dough on a table, wash your hands afterwards and never eat it, because it is not food and can upset your stomach.
fact: Stop using a toy that has been recalled (the maker has asked for it back) and tell a parent, because recalled toys have been found to be dangerous.
fact: Ask a parent before giving away old toys to younger children, a charity shop or a donation box.
exercise: Your baby brother is crawling near your Lego. What should you do? | Put the small pieces away out of his reach | Give him a few bricks to keep him busy | Let him play, because Lego is too big to swallow | Build him a tower to chew on
exercise: Why are marbles and balloons dangerous for children under three? | They can choke a little child who puts them in the mouth | They are far too heavy for babies and toddlers to lift up | They make babies sneeze | Babies under three are allergic to all glass and to all rubber
exercise: A button battery falls out of a toy. What do you do? | Give it to an adult at once | Put it in your pocket for later | Lick it to see if it still works | Leave it on the floor for someone else
exercise: Why is a swallowed button battery an emergency? | It can burn through the throat within hours | It makes the child glow in the dark for a week | It will just pass out the next day, so it does not matter | It only matters if the battery is brand new and full
exercise: Small strong magnets from a set are scattered on the floor. Why pick them all up? | Two swallowed magnets can stick together through the gut | Magnets damage the floor badly if they are left lying there | Magnets lose their power on carpet | They attract all the dust and the spiders in the house
exercise: A toy box says «Not for children under 3». What does it mean? | It may be a choking danger for toddlers | The toy is too clever for toddlers | It costs too much for young children | Only children aged exactly 3 may use it
exercise: Your toy truck breaks and has a sharp plastic edge. What do you do? | Stop playing with it and show an adult | Keep playing with it, but a bit more carefully | Sand the sharp edge down with your teeth | Give it to your little sister, who will not mind the edge
exercise: Where can you drive your remote-control car? | In the yard, the park or a quiet path | In the street, where it is smooth | Under parked cars on the road | Across the road to your friend's house
exercise: You get a toy drone. Where is it safe to fly it? | In an allowed open place with an adult, away from people | Over a busy beach full of people, so they can all watch | Near the airport, where there is space | Over the motorway, so you can film the cars going by
exercise: A yo-yo string is lying next to the baby's cot. What should you do? | Move it away, because cords can strangle a baby | Tie it to the side of the cot as a new toy for the baby | Leave it, because babies cannot hold strings | Wrap it round the baby's arm so it does not get lost
exercise: Your toddler cousin's ride-on car is next to the pool. What is right? | Move it away from the pool and stairs | Let him ride around the pool edge | Put it in the shallow end to clean it | Leave it, he will steer around the water
exercise: Water fight! Where should you aim? | At the body of people who are playing too | At the face, because that is funniest | At anyone walking past | At people who said they did not want to play
exercise: Why should you never take a realistic toy gun to the shops? | People, even police, can think it is real | It might get broken in the crowd | Shops do not allow any toys of any kind inside | Other children will all want to borrow it
exercise: What is the right way to play with slime? | On a table, washing your hands afterwards | On the sofa, so that it does not get cold | While eating, so you can taste it too | In your bed, just before you go to sleep
exercise: Your mum reads that your scooter has been recalled. What should you do? | Stop using it until it is checked | Ride it more, before it is taken away | Ignore it, recalls are only for cars | Sell it quickly to a friend before it goes
exercise: You have toys you no longer play with. What is a kind idea? | Ask a parent, then give them to a charity shop | Throw them all in the bin with the rest of the rubbish | Hide them away so that nobody else can use them | Give them to a toddler in the park without asking anyone
exercise: Your baby sister's teddy has a loose eye. What do you do? | Show an adult to sew it on or remove it | Let her play, because the eye is soft | Pull it off and leave it on the floor | Stick it back on with your sticky sweets
test: A girl in Toronto finds a coin-sized flat battery on the kitchen floor near her baby brother. What is right? | Give it to a parent at once, because swallowing it is an emergency | Put it in the toy box with the other toys | Leave it, because batteries are much too small to hurt anyone at all | Give it to her brother to play with
test: Which of these is most dangerous for a one-year-old? | A balloon that has popped into small pieces | A big soft cushion | A large wooden block | A cardboard picture book
test: A boy flies his drone over a crowded park. What is the problem? | Propellers can cut people if it falls | Drones scare the birds and clouds away | It uses up far too much of the battery | Nothing, because drones are always safe to fly anywhere
test: Your friend wants to chase his remote-control car into the road. What do you say? | Stop! Cars cannot see a small toy or you in time | Go, but only if it is a quiet road | Go, because toys are faster than cars | Wait until it reaches the other side of the road, then run after it
test: Why do toys have age labels? | They warn about choking and other dangers for young children | They tell you how clever you must be to play with the toy | They show how old the toy is | They are only there for the shop to keep its records
test: A teenager points a toy gun that looks real at people in the street as a joke. Why is this dangerous? | Someone, even the police, may think it is real | Toy guns can sometimes fire real bullets | It is only dangerous if it is loaded with caps | It wastes the toy's batteries
test: Which is true about water fights? | Aim at the body and only play with people who want to | The face is the best target | Everyone at the park must join in, whether they want to or not | It is fine to soak people walking past
test: A baby swallowed two little magnets from a game. What is true? | It is serious, because the magnets can stick together inside | The magnets will just pass out without doing any harm | One magnet is dangerous, but two magnets cancel each other out | It only matters if they are big
test: Your little brother has slime in his mouth. What is true? | Slime is not food and can upset his stomach | Slime is safe to eat as long as it is homemade | Slime only hurts if he swallows a lot | Slime is fine, because it is soft and squashy and has no bones
test: baby cousin's here n my lego's all over the floor | Pick up the small pieces so she can't choke | Leave it there, babies love playing with lego | Give her the smallest bricks, they're easy to hold | Only hide the red ones, babies always go for red
test: found a tiny round battery on the floor, can i play with it | No, give it to an adult, swallowing one is an emergency | Yes, they're too small to matter | Yes, if you don't lick it | Put it in your pocket n play with it later
test: new remote car, gonna race it down the street | No, only in the yard, park or a quiet path | Yes, streets are smooth and fast, perfect | Yes, if no cars are coming right now | Only along the edge of the pavement
test: got a toy gun looks totally real, takin it to the shops | No, leave it at home, people can think it's real | Yes, it's only a toy | Yes, if you keep it in your hand where people can see | Only if you paint it black first so it looks cooler
explain: A one-year-old in the USA found two small magnets that had fallen out of her brother's building set and swallowed them; they pulled together through her gut and she needed an operation.
explain: A girl in England gave her baby brother a toy car with a loose wheel; he put the wheel in his mouth and choked, and their mum had to do back blows until it came out.
fact: Two or more swallowed magnets can pull together through the walls of the gut and tear holes in it, so a swallowed magnet is always an emergency.
fact: Small parts are a choking danger for children under three, and pieces of burst balloon for children under eight.
test: lil sis wants my magnet set, she's 1 | Keep it away from her, swallowed magnets are an emergency | Give it, the magnets are too big to swallow | Give her just one magnet | Let her if you watch from the sofa
test: Why are bits of a burst balloon cleared up at once at a party with toddlers? | A small child can breathe them in and choke | They make the floor very slippery for the toddlers | They spoil all of the party photos afterwards | Burst balloon bits can pop again and frighten the little ones
test: baby put a lego bit in his mouth n now he's coughing | Get an adult right now | Give him a drink to wash it down | Wait and see if it passes | Shake him upside down yourself
test: did u hear a toddler swallowed 2 of those tiny strong magnets and they tore holes in his tummy? real lesson? | Pick up small magnets; swallowed ones are an emergency | One magnet is fine, it's only two that matter | Magnets pass through without harm | Only big magnets are dangerous, the tiny ones are fine
test: apparently a boy took his toy gun to the shopping centre and armed police surrounded him. what's the true lesson? | Never take a realistic toy gun to a public place | Toy guns are fine to take anywhere | Just paint it a bright colour first and then it's fine | Police can always tell a toy
test: my mate says his baby sister swallowed a Lego brick and it came out fine so small parts are no big deal. right? | No, small parts can choke babies and toddlers | Yes, babies can swallow anything | Yes, if they're made of plastic | Yes, Lego is made for babies to chew on
exam: Identify the purpose of age labels such as «3+» upon toys. | To warn of choking and other dangers for young children | To indicate the difficulty of the toy | To show the minimum price at which the toy may be sold | To show the age of the toy itself
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises two swallowed magnets? | They may join through the gut wall and cause serious injury | They pass through the body without any effect whatever | They are harmless if small | They dissolve in the stomach
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice in respect of a recalled toy. | Its use ceases and a parent is informed | It is used only at weekends | It is given away to a younger child | It is repaired with tape and used
exam: Ascertain the place where a remote-control car may properly be driven. | In a yard, park or quiet path, never in the street | In the street when traffic is light | On the pavement beside a busy road | In a car park among moving cars
